{
  "track": "mission",
  "allocation_reasoning": "The epistemic infrastructure (confidence scoring, review protocols, refined Lorentz bounds) is now operationalized (T1 and T8 done). The next logical step is to execute T2: catalogue the mathematical limits of knowability. This builds directly on established findings F0001 (Gödel) and F0002 (Turing), and will produce verifiable reference findings for Gödel, Turing, Tarski, Chaitin and related theorems with explicit domain boundaries. This is a concrete, achievable task for one session using web_search and arxiv_search.",
  "roadmap_ops": [
    {"op": "set_status", "id": "T2", "status": "active", "reason": "Epistemic infrastructure ready; proceed with cataloguing formal limits of knowability as the next foundational milestone."}
  ],
  "task_id": "T2",
  "task_brief": "Compile a verified reference of foundational undecidability, incompleteness, and uncomputability theorems with explicit domain boundaries. Research and produce findings for: (1) Gödel's First and Second Incompleteness Theorems (precise conditions: consistent, effectively axiomatized, extends Robinson arithmetic Q or PA); (2) Turing's Halting Problem undecidability (and the unsolvability of the Entscheidungsproblem); (3) Tarski's Undefinability of Truth Theorem; (4) Chaitin's Incompleteness Theorem / Algorithmic Information Theory limits (Omega constant, incompleteness for Kolmogorov complexity); (5) Rice's Theorem (non-trivial semantic properties of programs undecidable); (6) Post's Correspondence Problem undecidability; (7) Matiyasevich's Theorem (Hilbert's 10th Problem unsolvable). For each: state the theorem precisely, specify the formal system/domain assumptions, give the refutation condition, cite authoritative sources (textbooks, original papers, or Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entries), and assign confidence per F0009 (deductive mathematical claims). Output 5-8 findings as verifiable claims.",
  "why_this_task": "T2 is the next proposed task under M1 (Epistemic Infrastructure). It directly builds on F0001 and F0002 which are already at confidence 1.0. Completing it will give us a rigorous, citable catalogue of formal limits that prevents wasted effort on undecidable problems and serves as a dependency anchor for all future work. The worker (Gemini) has the right tools (web_search, arxiv_search, wikipedia_search) to retrieve precise theorem statements and authoritative citations in one session."
}

{
  "claim_reviews": [
    {
      "claim": 1,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Gödel's Second Incompleteness Theorem is stated precisely with correct hypotheses (consistent, recursively axiomatized, interprets Q/PA, Hilbert-Bernays-Löb conditions). Domain boundaries correctly exclude decidable theories (Presburger, real closed fields), inconsistent theories, and non-effective theories. Refutation condition is operationally specific. Sources include original paper, standard textbook, and mechanical verification in Isabelle/HOL (Paulson 2014) and Coq (O'Connor 2005), justifying confidence 1.0 per F0009. Type 'verifiable' is correct. Builds_on dependencies (F0001, F0009) are appropriate."
    },
    {
      "claim": 2,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Unsolvability of the Entscheidungsproblem is correctly stated: first-order validity is undecidable. Domain boundaries correctly identify decidable fragments (propositional, monadic FO, FO2, Presburger). Refutation condition is precise. Sources include Church 1936, Turing 1936, and Sipser textbook. Textbook consensus with no known gaps justifies confidence 1.0 per F0009. Type 'verifiable' correct. Builds_on (F0002, F0009) appropriate."
    },
    {
      "claim": 3,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Tarski's Undefinability of Truth Theorem is precisely stated: no formula Tr(x) in T defines truth for T when T extends Q. Domain boundaries correctly note metalanguage hierarchies and weaker systems. Refutation condition is exact. Sources include Tarski 1936, Smith 2013, SEP entry, and mechanical verification in Isabelle/HOL and Coq. Confidence 1.0 justified per F0009. Type 'verifiable' correct. Builds_on (F0001, F0009) appropriate."
    },
    {
      "claim": 4,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Chaitin's Incompleteness Theorem and Ω properties are correctly stated: existence of constant c such that T cannot prove K(x) > c, and Ω is Martin-Löf random with initial n bits requiring n - O(1) bits. Domain boundaries correctly note prefix-free machines, effective theories, and exceptions (oracles, upper bounds). Refutation condition is specific. Sources include Chaitin 1974, 1975, and Li & Vitányi textbook. Textbook consensus justifies confidence 1.0 per F0009. Type 'verifiable' correct. Builds_on (F0001, F0002, F0009) appropriate."
    },
    {
      "claim": 5,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Rice's Theorem is precisely stated: non-trivial semantic properties of partial computable functions are undecidable. Domain boundaries correctly distinguish semantic vs syntactic properties and trivial properties. Refutation condition is exact. Sources include Rice 1953, Rogers 1987, and mechanical verification in Coq/Isabelle (Forster & Larchey-Wendling 2019). Confidence 1.0 justified per F0009. Type 'verifiable' correct. Builds_on (F0002, F0009) appropriate."
    },
    {
      "claim": 6,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Post's Correspondence Problem undecidability is correctly stated for |Σ| ≥ 2. Domain boundaries correctly identify decidable cases: unary alphabet (|Σ|=1), n ≤ 2 pairs, bounded-length matching. Refutation condition is precise. Sources include Post 1946, Hopcroft/Motwani/Ullman, and mechanical verification in Coq (Larchey-Wendling & Forster 2019). Confidence 1.0 justified per F0009. Type 'verifiable' correct. Builds_on (F0002, F0009) appropriate."
    },
    {
      "claim": 7,
      "verdict": "accept",
      "reason": "Matiyasevich's Theorem (MRDP) is correctly stated: every c.e. set is Diophantine; Hilbert's 10th Problem unsolvable. Domain boundaries correctly note decidable subclasses: linear/single-variable/two-variable quadratic Diophantine equations, and equations over ℝ (Tarski) or ℂ (Gröbner bases). Refutation condition is exact. Sources include Matiyasevich 1970, Davis 1973, and mechanical verification in Coq (Larchey-Wendling & Forster 2019). Confidence 1.0 justified per F0009. Type 'verifiable' correct. Builds_on (F0002, F0009) appropriate."
    }
  ],
  "update_reviews": [],
  "task_complete": true,
  "appeal_check": "No claim appears shaped to impress rather than to be true. All seven theorems are stated with mathematical precision, explicit domain boundaries, honest refutation conditions, and confidence assignments that follow the declared rubric (F0009). The worker has not exaggerated scope, omitted known exceptions, or used rhetorical flourishes. The work is a sober, verified reference catalogue as requested.",
  "disagreements": [],
  "next_step": "Proceed to Task [T3] (Taxonomy of major open questions across the verifiable/conceptual boundary) or initiate [M2]/[M3] tasks focusing on formal deductive core and physical information/thermodynamic bounds, as suggested by the worker."
}
